nerable um and i  i think circumstances have changed i  mean think about my own family  a few weeks ago we were i think clear  that we would meet up  because you know my mom and dad have  been longing to get the family  together i think today not just because  the cases of the virus are rising in  some parts of the country  but also because of the good news of the  vaccine has  changed some people's perspective  because many families think now  well you know what by january or  february uh the elderly members of my  family may well be vaccinated  and so why not wait and get the family  back together  you know later on in 2021 and that's the  sort of judgment i think people will be  making across the country but would  could you be explicit just for a second  if you have  people in the categories that were  shielding for example  uh during the first part of this  pandemic is it your view that we should  not be spending christmas with them  well we're not telling people how to  come to this  decision we can give guidance the chief  medical officer obviously has set out  his own views  and he's been clear that he would  exercise great caution  in bringing people together particularly  those who have certain  vulnerabilities but you can't legislate  for every eventuality you've got to  ask people in the end to use their own  personal judgment  and christmas is a fairly unique time of  year  many people have been longing to to come  together yeah and so we're offering  people obviously  the ability to do that but asking them  to use their own good judgment  but but again the situation does appear  pretty confusingly take the yougov  snap poll suggesting i think it's 50 57  of people actually think the christmas  window should be dropped add into the  mix  the british medical journal the health  service journal  legions of scientists all suggesting  that this christmas bubble will lead  directly to  a third wave and deaths well the legal  frameworks i say is there people 